 ["Scales both the bed and the strikes for %s, before the two shared strengths above."] = "Scales both the bed and the strikes for %s, before the two shared strengths above.",
 ["Scales this cue on top of the overall intensity on the Pulse page."] = "Scales this cue on top of the overall intensity on the Pulse page.",
 ["School lockouts are frequent in melee-heavy content. Off by default because a cue every kick you eat can crowd out the ones that mean you cannot act at all."] = "School lockouts are frequent in melee-heavy content. Off by default because a cue every kick you eat can crowd out the ones that mean you cannot act at all.",
 ["Scored by how long you were airborne, not by fall damage — that number is unreadable. A short hop stays silent below the threshold."] = "Scored by how long you were airborne, not by fall damage — that number is unreadable. A short hop stays silent below the threshold.",
 ["Search %d cues..."] = "Search %d cues...",
 ["Second steepening point — the steepest segment of all, climbing to heartRateCritical by zero breath. Requested live 2026-09-16 as ~15% breath remaining."] = "Second steepening point — the steepest segment of all, climbing to heartRateCritical by zero breath. Requested live 2026-09-16 as ~15% breath remaining.",
 ["Second-harmonic Stokes drift shaping. 0.0 is a pure symmetric sine wave; higher values create a sharp, steep crest and an elongated trough, matching real fluid gravity waves."] = "Second-harmonic Stokes drift shaping. 0.0 is a pure symmetric sine wave; higher values create a sharp, steep crest and an elongated trough, matching real fluid gravity waves.",
 ["Second-harmonic content, which is what makes a stroke feel like pull-then-glide rather than a symmetric swell. Six earlier tuning rounds all felt \"rough\" because they modulated the amplitude of a constant; a pure sine cannot express this asymmetry and that is most likely why. 0 reverts to a plain swell."] = "Second-harmonic content, which is what makes a stroke feel like pull-then-glide rather than a symmetric swell. Six earlier tuning rounds all felt \"rough\" because they modulated the amplitude of a constant; a pure sine cannot express this asymmetry and that is most likely why. 0 reverts to a plain swell.",
 ["Second: find out what your controller can do"] = "Second: find out what your controller can do",
